{
  "id" : "ab34-d3ma",
  "name" : "Special Education Circuit Breaker Reimbursements",
  "assetType" : "dataset",
  "attribution" : "Department of Elementary and Secondary Education",
  "averageRating" : 0,
  "category" : "Finance and Budget",
  "createdAt" : 1782228605,
  "description" : "This dataset includes reimbursement amounts provided to Massachusetts public school districts through the Commonwealth's Special Education Circuit Breaker program (\"circuit breaker\") starting with fiscal year 2006. It also includes expenditure amounts reported to the Department of Elementary and Secondary Education (DESE) on annual circuit breaker claims. \n\nCircuit breaker program reimburses districts for qualified high-cost special education expenditures in two categories:  instruction/tuition and transportation. Instruction/tuition has been reimbursed since the program’s inception. Transportation reimbursement was added with the Student Opportunity Act (beginning fiscal year 2022). \n\nDistricts report costs annually through a claiming process. Expenses over the annual per-student claim threshold are reimbursed quarterly, in the following fiscal year, and at a consistent percentage statewide dependent on available budget. \n\nPer statute (M.G.L. <a href=\"https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXII/Chapter71b/Section5a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Chapter 71B Section 5A</a>), reimbursements are calculated first in the instruction/tuition category. Then, any money left in the state circuit breaker budget line is calculated for transportation reimbursement. Additionally, each year the per-student claim threshold increases based on inflation according to a defined formula. \n\nCircuit breaker reimburses through multiple program components:<ul><li><b>Quarterly payments</b> are provided four times per year and are reimbursements for expenses incurred in the prior fiscal year. Expenses in the instruction and tuition category have been reimbursed since the program's inception. Beginning with the implementation of the Student Opportunity Act (FY22 payments), expenses for out-of-district transportation are also reimbursed.</li><li><b>Extraordinary Relief payments</b> are provided once a year to districts showing a significant (greater than 25%) increase in instruction and tuition expenses over the prior year. This reimbursement is provided to qualified districts in the same fiscal year expenses were incurred.</li><li><b>Additional/Supplemental payments</b> are provided in atypical scenarios, such as in years when the legislature enacts additional circuit breaker reimbursement mechanisms or authorizes supplemental funding for distribution.</li></ul>More information regarding the circuit breaker program can be found on the <a href=\"https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/circuitbreaker/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">DESE circuit breaker website</a>, including historic information of <a href=\"https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/circuitbreaker/rates.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"> claim thresholds and percentage reimbursement rates</a>. In addition to the standard reimbursement percentages indicated in the chart, students claimed with special indicators (homeless, state ward or abandoned) are reimbursed at 100% over the claim threshold.",
